  <h2>What Are the Longji Rice Terraces?</h2>
  <p>The Longji (Dragon''s Backbone) Rice Terraces are a vast network of stepped rice paddies built into the mountains over 650 years ago by the Zhuang and Yao ethnic minorities. Located about 80 km north of Guilin, they''re a half-day trip from Guilin or a full-day excursion from Yangshuo. The terraces change dramatically with the seasons: flooded and reflective in spring, lush green in summer, golden in autumn, and sometimes dusted with snow in winter.</p>

  <h2>Is It Worth the Detour?</h2>
  <p>Yes, if you have at least 4 days in the region. With only 3 days, the travel time (2 hours each way from Guilin) eats into your Yangshuo time. If you have 4-5 days, Longji is a fantastic addition that shows a completely different side of the Guilin region — ethnic minority culture and agricultural landscapes rather than river-and-karst scenery. See our <a href="/guilin-yangshuo-itinerary-5-day" class="text-accent underline">5-day itinerary</a> for how to fit it in.</p>

  <h2>The Two Main Areas</h2>
  <h3>Ping''an Village</h3>
  <p>The more developed and accessible area. Two main viewpoints — <strong>Seven Stars with Moon</strong> and <strong>Nine Dragons and Five Tigers</strong> — connected by stone paths. Good for first-time visitors. The village itself has restaurants, guesthouses, and Yao women who''ll offer to pose for photos (for a small fee of 10-20 RMB). The walk between the two viewpoints takes about 40 minutes. Ping''an is best for a day trip — you can see both viewpoints and be back in Guilin by evening.</p>
  <h3>Jinkeng (Dazhai) Village</h3>
  <p>Larger, more dramatic, and less crowded. The terraces here are bigger and steeper. Three viewpoints — <strong>West Hill Music from Paradise</strong> (for sunrise), <strong>Thousands of Layers to Heaven</strong> (the highest), and <strong>Golden Buddha Peak</strong> (for sunset) — accessible by hiking paths or a cable car. Jinkeng is better for serious photographers and hikers, but it''s farther from the entrance and takes more time. If you''re staying overnight, Jinkeng is the better choice.</p>

  <h2>Hiking Between Viewpoints</h2>
  <h3>Ping''an Routes</h3>
  <p>The two viewpoints are connected by well-marked stone paths. Allow 1.5-2 hours to visit both at a comfortable pace. The paths go through the terraces themselves — you''re walking along narrow ridges between flooded rice paddies. Wear shoes with good grip; the stones can be slippery when wet.</p>
  <h3>Jinkeng Routes</h3>
  <p>The three viewpoints form a rough triangle. Walking between all three takes 3-4 hours. The cable car runs from the village entrance to Golden Buddha Peak (60 RMB one-way, 100 RMB round trip). A popular strategy: cable car up to Golden Buddha for sunrise, hike down to West Hill, then walk to Thousands of Layers for the afternoon light.</p>

