  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-stone-400">The rice planting happens in late May, and the harvest in early October. The terraces are at their most photogenic in the 2-3 weeks after flooding (late May to mid-June) and the 2-3 weeks before harvest (late September to early October).</p>

  <h2>Practical Info</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Entrance fee:</strong> 80 RMB (includes access to all villages and viewpoints)</li>
    <li><strong>Cable car (Jinkeng only):</strong> 60 RMB one-way, 100 RMB round trip</li>
    <li><strong>Getting there:</strong> Direct bus from Guilin Bus Station (near Guilin Railway Station) to Longji — 2-2.5 hours, ~40 RMB. Buses run every 60-90 minutes from 7 AM to 4 PM. From Yangshuo, take a bus to Guilin first (1.5h, 25 RMB) then transfer. A private car from Guilin costs 400-600 RMB round trip — worth it for 2+ people.</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to start:</strong> Leave Guilin by 7:30 AM to arrive at the terraces by 10 AM. This gives you 4-5 hours of hiking before the afternoon bus back.</li>
    <li><strong>Stay overnight?</strong> Recommended for photographers wanting sunrise. Guesthouses in Ping''an and Jinkeng run 100-400 RMB/night. Book on Trip.com or call ahead — mountain guesthouses fill up during peak seasons.</li>
    <li><strong>Walking difficulty:</strong> Moderate. The paths are stone steps — lots of them. Not suitable for anyone with knee problems or limited mobility. The cable car at Jinkeng provides wheelchair access to one viewpoint.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Local Culture</h2>
  <p>The terraces are still worked by Zhuang and Yao minority families who live in the villages year-round. You''ll see farmers tending the paddies, water buffalo being led along the paths, and women in traditional embroidered clothing. The Yao women are famous for their long hair (some over 1.5 meters) and will demonstrate hair-combing for visitors. They sell handmade embroidery, silver jewelry, and local tea. Bargaining is expected — start at 50% of the asking price.</p>
  <p>Consider eating at a local guesthouse rather than the larger tourist restaurants. Dishes like <strong>bamboo rice</strong> (rice and pork stuffed into bamboo and roasted), <strong>smoked pork with pickled vegetables</strong>, and <strong>wild mountain vegetables</strong> are regional specialties. A meal for two runs 60-100 RMB.</p>

